Source: mptcp-bpf-weight
Section: net
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Yannick Chabanois <contact@openmptcprouter.com>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13), clang, llvm, libbpf-dev, linux-libc-dev
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://www.openmptcprouter.com
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: mptcp-bpf-weight
Architecture: any
# No ${shlibs:Depends}: the installed artifact is a BPF-target ELF object
# loaded by bpftool/the kernel, not a dynamically-linked host executable -
# see mptcp-bpf-dscp's identical note.
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, bpftool, procps
Description: MPTCP BPF single-winner weighted scheduler (server/VPS side)
 BPF struct_ops MPTCP scheduler that always sends on the single
 highest-weight active subflow, weighted either by local endpoint IP
 (router-side, see the OpenWrt mptcp-bpf-weight package in
 openmptcprouter-feeds) or by MPTCP remote endpoint id (server/VPS-side:
 every subflow here shares the same local IP, so weights key on the
 address id the router assigned to the originating WAN instead).
 .
 This is the Debian/server counterpart of that OpenWrt package: same BPF
 program, packaged to run on the VPS half of an OpenMPTCProuter multipath
 link. Ships a systemd unit that loads and pins the scheduler at boot and
 sets it as net.mptcp.scheduler. Install mptcp-weight-manager to actually
 configure weights.

Package: mptcp-weight-manager
Architecture: all
# mptcp-bpf-weight-rr is now its own source package (see that package's
# debian/ directory) - version-pinning against it here isn't possible
# across source packages, so only the mptcp-bpf-weight alternative is
# pinned to this source's version.
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, bpftool, mptcp-bpf-weight (= ${source:Version}) | mptcp-bpf-weight-rr
Description: CLI to manage the mptcp-bpf-weight(-rr) schedulers' weights
 mptcp-scheduler-weight.sh: show/set/del per-endpoint weights in the
 mptcp-bpf-weight/mptcp-bpf-weight-rr schedulers' shared BPF maps (both
 schedulers use the same endpoint_weights/weight_remote_id maps, so one
 manager script serves either - mptcp-bpf-weight-rr is packaged
 separately, see its own source package).
 .
 On this (server/VPS) package, use 'mptcp-scheduler-weight.sh set id <N>
 <weight>' where <N> is the MPTCP endpoint id the router assigned to the
 WAN that should carry that weight (network.<iface>.ip4table on the
 router, kept stable across reconnects). The router-side 'set <interface>
 <weight>' form also works here but is normally useless on a VPS with a
 single local IP.
